3. Core stage geometry — one platform, three sections

This is the most important requirement.

At the beginning of every match, the arena must look and play like one enormous uninterrupted block of ice.

Conceptually:

         ONE CONTINUOUS WALKABLE ICE SURFACE
  _______________________________________________
 |               |                 |             |
 |               |                 |             |
 |   LEFT ICE    |   CENTRE ICE    |  RIGHT ICE  |
 |   BREAKABLE   |  INDESTRUCTIBLE |  BREAKABLE  |
 |               |                 |             |
 |_______________|_________________|_____________|
       ICE              ICE             ICE
    FOUNDATION       FOUNDATION      FOUNDATION

The vertical divisions in this diagram represent internal boundaries for implementation. They must not look like open gaps, separate floating platforms, or large visible seams before damage begins.

Initial appearance

All three sections must:

* Be physically adjacent without horizontal gaps.
* Share exactly the same walkable top elevation.
* Have matching surface textures and ice thickness.
* Form a visually continuous top, front face and foundation.
* Permit seamless walking, running, dashing, jumping, shielding and combat across their joins.
* Use Alva’s existing one-point perspective.
* Have no artificial walls, bumps or collision barriers at their internal boundaries.

The stage must look like a massive frozen landmass, not three small floating rectangles placed side by side.

Foundation

Follow the visual principles of Yerma and Sol-Noir.

Jökull must have a substantial visible foundation extending downward beneath the playable ice surface.

This foundation should look like a colossal glacier: layered blue-white ice, deep turquoise frozen strata, trapped bubbles, crevasses and darker ancient ice beneath the surface.

Unlike Adamaris’s flat continuous ground, the entire ice platform should visibly stand above an open frozen abyss.

Each outer section must have its own detachable foundation. When that section collapses, its complete volume breaks away, not just the thin walkable surface.

Geometry and sizing

Use the existing stages as the reference for combat scale.

Suggested initial proportions:

* Centre: approximately 60–65% of total initial platform width.
* Left: approximately 17–20%.
* Right: approximately 17–20%.
* All sections share one exact surface height.
* Spawn both fighters safely on the indestructible centre.

The exact world coordinates should be tuned to Alva’s existing jump, Dash, recovery, camera and Void rules.

Important technical distinction: Do not set Jökull to ground: { type: 'continuous' }.

In Alva, that setting creates uninterrupted ground beyond the Void. It would leave invisible or unintended support after the glacier wings disappear.

Instead, implement a composite finite stage whose initial footprint contains three contiguous, independently collidable solid sections.

The map’s overall combat bounds and Void calculations should still account for the full initial footprint, including both side sections.

If required, introduce a general-purpose composite-floor or destructible-solid extension to StageCollision, preserving every existing stage’s behaviour.

4. Structural rules for the three sections

Centre section

The centre is permanent and indestructible.

It must:

* Never take structural damage.
* Never crack from gameplay impacts.
* Never lose collision.
* Never fall.
* Never disappear during blizzards.
* Remain the same size and position throughout the match.
* Always serve as stable footing and a recovery destination.

The centre can have purely decorative ancient ice patterns, but never damage-state cracks that imply it will collapse.

Left section

The left section has its own persistent structural integrity.

* Starts fully intact.
* Accumulates one unit of damage per qualifying new landing or entry event.
* Reaches destruction after 15 counted contacts by default.
* Visually develops increasing damage.
* Eventually breaks completely away.
* Never reforms during the current match.

Right section

Identical rules to the left, but completely independent.

Its landing counter, structural damage, animations and destruction state must not depend on the left section.

Example:

If the left section has received 12 qualifying landings and the right section has received 3, the left must appear heavily cracked while the right remains relatively intact.

If the left collapses, the right must continue functioning normally.

No damage should propagate across the indestructible centre.

5. Define exactly what counts as a landing

The ice should react to both fighters equally.

The following must count as a qualifying contact when it establishes new footing on a breakable side:

* Normal jump landings.
* High-jump landings.
* Landings after an air jump.
* Landings following an air Dash.
* Landings after being launched or knocked back.
* Returning to a side platform after previously leaving it.
* Falling onto it from above.
* Directly transferring onto a side from another section, including running or dashing across the internal seam, once the fighter’s support transitions to that side.
* Landing during a blizzard.
* Any other supported gameplay state that genuinely establishes a new contact.

Do not restrict the mechanic to pressing the Jump button.

Contact counting safeguards

Damage must be applied once per new support/contact event, not once per physics frame.

A fighter standing still on a side for several seconds must not continuously damage it.

Walking around while maintaining support on that same section must not generate repeated hits.

Use the actual grounded surface identity and contact transitions to determine when a new landing or entry has occurred.

Account for the fact that Alva’s existing body.landed is true for airborne-to-grounded transitions but may not detect a grounded transfer from the centre to another section.

Explicitly handle these grounded support changes.

Each fighter should have correctly maintained contact state to prevent false increments from collision jitter.

A wall collision against the side or underside of the ice must not count as landing on its top.

If two fighters independently land on the same side during the same simulation step, both valid contacts may count, subject to the destruction threshold.

Never allow the counter to exceed its configured maximum.

The system must remain deterministic and independent of rendering frame rate.

6. Structural damage progression

Default durability:

MAX_ICE_CONTACTS = 15

Make the threshold configurable in one clearly named data module instead of scattering literal values throughout the implementation.

Every qualifying contact should cause a small physical and visual response.

Examples of escalating damage:

Count	Required visual progression
0	Clean, intact, thick ice
1–3	Fine hairline fractures near impact areas
4–6	More visible branching cracks and small frost fragments
7–9	Significant cracks spreading across the top and front face
10–12	Deep fractures, visible internal separation and stronger impact reactions
13–14	Critical instability, large branching cracks and subtle trembling
15	Full structural failure and collapse sequence

The damage must be cumulative.

Do not simply switch between an intact sprite and a broken sprite.

Create a genuinely progressive cracking system where additional damage becomes increasingly obvious.

Damage visuals

A qualifying landing should produce:

* A crisp ice-impact reaction.
* A new fracture or an extension of existing fractures.
* Tiny chips or ice crystals bursting outward.
* A subtle local vibration.
* A brief icy highlight showing stress spreading from the impact.
* An appropriate sound cue if it fits the existing audio system.

The fracture network must be persistent across frames and must not regenerate randomly on each render.

Use stable, seeded or otherwise deterministic geometry for damage patterns.

The top surface and visible front foundation should both reflect the section’s actual damage state.

Cracks should gradually become darker, wider, longer, and more interconnected as collapse approaches.

They must remain legible even with constant snow particles in the scene.

7. Full ice-breaking animation

When either section receives its fifteenth qualifying contact, initiate a complete, unmistakable breaking sequence.

A simple instant disappearance or opacity fade is unacceptable.

Phase A — critical fracture

Recommended duration: approximately 0.2–0.35 seconds.

* The section visibly shakes.
* Major cracks rapidly expand.
* Fracture lines illuminate with cold white and cyan highlights.
* Small ice chips jump from the surface.
* The affected section appears unstable.
* The centre and opposite side remain unaffected.

Phase B — structural separation

Recommended duration: approximately 0.2–0.4 seconds.

* The broken section separates along its inner boundary.
* Its upper ice slab splits into several larger irregular pieces.
* Deep fractures extend through the visible foundation.
* The foundation visibly tears apart, exposing darker blue ice inside.
* The section begins losing structural coherence.

Phase C — collapse

Recommended duration: approximately 0.6–1.2 seconds.

* The broken section separates completely from the centre.
* Large chunks rotate and fall downward.
* Several smaller shards scatter and fall at different speeds.
* Fine snow and ice particles trail behind the falling chunks.
* The newly exposed edge of the permanent centre looks naturally broken, not like a hard rectangular cut.
* The debris descends toward the Void and is eventually culled.

Use gravity-like acceleration and modest angular motion to make the collapse physically convincing.

A small camera shake may accompany the main fracture, respecting reduced-motion settings.

Collision requirements

The collision must disappear in exact synchronization with the section’s physical separation.

Do not leave invisible ground supporting fighters after the ice visually falls.

Do not remove support long before the break becomes visually apparent.

During the short critical-fracture anticipation, the section may remain solid. Once structural separation begins, its collision must be disabled and any fighter supported by it must begin falling, unless another active section legitimately supports them.

Use or extend releaseGround as appropriate.

Fighters should retain their normal movement, jumping, air-dash, recovery and combat behaviour during the collapse.

Do not automatically eliminate a fighter because an ice section collapsed. The fighter should physically fall and may recover under Alva’s existing rules. Elimination occurs only upon reaching the actual Void boundary.

The falling pieces are visual debris, not independent damaging projectiles.

Permanent destruction

Once destroyed, a side section must remain destroyed for the rest of the current match.

It must not regenerate, reset after a respawn, or automatically reappear after a timer.

After both sides collapse, only the centre section remains playable.

The resulting gaps must contain genuine open air and must be handled correctly by physics, projectiles, shadows, CPU movement and recoveries.

8. Jökull-specific hazard architecture

Classify both destructible sections as structural stage hazards.

However, do not attempt to implement them as ordinary Asperitas cloud hazards.

The existing PlatformHazards system assumes that its moving or disappearing platforms remain spatially separated from the main stage and may eventually reform.

Jökull deliberately requires the opposite:

* Its sections initially touch.
* They are substantial blocks with foundations.
* Their collision is aligned with the main surface.
* They never move before breaking.
* Their destruction is permanent within the match.

Implement an appropriate controller, for example IceSectionController, within a dedicated Jökull hazard system or a clean extension of the existing stage-hazard architecture.

Each breakable section should have explicit runtime state, including:

* Section identity.
* Current contact count.
* Maximum contact count.
* Damage progression.
* Intact, critical, breaking and destroyed states.
* Collision activation.
* Break-animation timing.
* Generated fracture geometry.
* Active debris particles.
* Stable render state.

The map definition must remain immutable. Every Arena and StagePreview should own fresh runtime state.

Ensure section collision identity remains reliable for fighters entering from the central block.

Update all relevant collision queries, including horizontal solid collision, vertical landing, support detection, shadows, and CPU queries, so destroyed solids are truly inactive everywhere.

Never leave stale static-surface cache entries that cause the CPU to plan landings onto destroyed ice.

9. Blizzard event — 25% random chance

Jökull has a second, independent hazard: Blizzard.

This is a significant temporary snowstorm that obscures the battlefield and reduces visibility.

Probability

Use an exact, configurable probability:

BLIZZARD_CHANCE = 0.25

This represents a 25% chance whenever the weather controller performs an eligible event check.

Do not roll every rendered frame or fixed simulation step.

Instead, use independent weather-event intervals.

Recommended initial tuning:

* Opening calm: 5–8 seconds.
* Event checks: once every 12–18 seconds while eligible.
* Probability per check: 25%.
* Blizzard duration: approximately 6–9 seconds.
* Transition buildup: approximately 1–1.5 seconds.
* Transition out: approximately 1–1.5 seconds.
* No overlapping blizzards.

Adjust the duration and intervals if playtesting indicates a better balance, but retain the exact 25% chance per eligible event check.

A failed roll leaves the stage in its normal weather state until the next eligible check.

A successful roll triggers one blizzard event.

There must be no forced alternation between blizzards and normal weather.

Weather states

Use a clear deterministic state machine, such as:

calm → warning → blizzard → clearing → calm

The controller’s random stream must be seeded using Alva’s existing random utilities.

It must not depend on render frequency, animation frames, the CPU’s random stream, or structural-damage counters.

Blizzard buildup

Before the blizzard reaches full strength:

* Wind intensity gradually increases.
* Snowflakes begin moving faster and more horizontally.
* Distant scenery becomes hazier.
* Cold fog grows across the stage.
* The atmosphere visibly turns whiter.
* Wind gusts build toward the main event.

This must feel like an approaching storm, not an instantly appearing white rectangle.

Full blizzard

At peak intensity:

* Dense snow sweeps across the screen in multiple directions.
* Snowflakes move strongly with the wind.
* Large near-camera snow particles streak across the view.
* Fine distant snow forms a textured white haze.
* Gusts and drifting snow create layered movement.
* Visibility is significantly reduced.
* The sky, mountains and distant scenery become difficult to distinguish.
* The platforms and fighters remain partially visible through the storm.

The storm should be challenging without becoming completely opaque.

Target a substantial loss of environmental clarity while preserving enough visibility to avoid making combat unplayable.

Keep gameplay HUD, score, timer, menus and essential controls readable. Apply the whiteout within the battle Canvas rather than obscuring interface elements unnecessarily.

Blizzard clearing

When the event ends:

* The strongest gusts diminish.
* Snow density gradually decreases.
* Fog begins to clear.
* Distant background elements reappear.
* The atmosphere returns smoothly to its ordinary snowfall.

Do not abruptly switch from dense whiteout to a clear scene.

Gameplay rules

The blizzard itself is a visibility hazard, not a damage attack.

It should not:

* Apply fighter damage.
* Modify Launch Point.
* Automatically stun fighters.
* Force fighters into the Void.
* Change the structural durability of the ice.

The base requirement is increased visual difficulty, not altered movement physics.

Wind must be clearly represented visually and atmospherically, but do not introduce strong random forces on fighters unless explicitly designed, balanced and justified separately.

Blizzards and platform damage must be able to occur simultaneously.

An ice section must still receive damage and break correctly during a blizzard.

The blizzard must not hide critical cracking or collapse cues so completely that players cannot understand what happened.

