11. Damage visuals and rendering synchronization

The Jökull theme must render directly from the live state of the ice hazard controllers.

It must never maintain an unrelated copy of structural damage.

The visual representation must match collision state.

Examples:

* Left at 4 contacts: mild left-side cracks.
* Right at 12 contacts: severe right-side cracking.
* Centre: no structural damage.
* Left destroyed: no left walkable surface.
* Right destroyed: no right walkable surface.
* Both destroyed: only centre remains.
* Blizzard active: all currently existing ice sections remain rendered beneath the storm.

Damage must also correctly affect the front and side faces of the glacier.

The theme must not draw a completely intact giant block after one section has already fallen away.

When an ice section collapses, reveal a naturally broken edge on the remaining glacier, especially at its connection with the permanent centre.

All fragment and fracture animation coordinates must remain consistent with the game’s world-to-screen transformation and camera movement.

Use simulation-driven destruction events and animation state. Rendering must not modify gameplay damage or trigger additional break events.

