The starting stage should use the same general camera scale and framing principles as Yerma and Sol-Noir.

At match start, players should see the full glacier with both side sections and sufficient surrounding air.

Account for the overall starting platform footprint when establishing stage, camera and Void bounds.

The indestructible centre must remain fully inside the usable Void area, including overtime.

Ensure that:

* Both intact side sections are playable.
* Falling off an exterior ledge follows standard Void rules.
* Falling into a newly created gap follows standard gravity and recovery rules.
* Fighters can jump back from newly exposed edges if their abilities permit it.
* No destroyed section creates an invisible wall.
* No section extends into unintended kill zones at match start.
* Projectiles and summons continue functioning according to existing rules.
* Collision and actual drawn surfaces align.
* Camera framing behaves correctly after one or both sides collapse.
* Overtime’s closing Void remains compatible with the changing stage topology.

Do not change universal character movement values to compensate for the new stage.

Use existing movement and recovery behaviour when balancing the dimensions of the ice sections.

13. CPU awareness and hazard intelligence

Update Alva’s existing stage intelligence so CPUs understand Jökull.

The CPU must recognize:

* The intact central section.
* Each active breakable side.
* The currently available landing and walking surfaces.
* The remaining structural integrity of each outer section, when visually communicated.
* A side section that is critically cracked.
* The beginning of a collapse.
* Permanently missing ice sections.
* Newly exposed ledges and dangerous gaps.

The existing StageModel caches static surfaces and derives main-stage ledges from the stage’s original geometry.

That behaviour must be reviewed carefully because Jökull’s collision topology changes during a match.

Do not allow permanently destroyed sides to remain cached as valid landing targets or safe walking areas.

Use live collision information where needed.

CPU decisions

CPUs should still be willing to fight on the outer sections while they exist.

They should not irrationally avoid the entire stage from the beginning.

However, when a side becomes critically unstable, a CPU should be capable of recognizing the visible danger and attempting to move to safety.

CPU response should respect existing difficulty and reaction rules.

The CPU should not receive supernatural advance knowledge of the exact next random blizzard event.

A blizzard can reduce the player’s visibility without requiring the introduction of new unbalanced CPU penalties.

Both CPUs in Watch Mode must experience the exact same stage destruction and weather state.

